global_middleware_order_in_router (router.go:58-96, observed exactly):
  1. chimw.RequestID                  -- assigns X-Request-ID, populated into ctx (chimw.GetReqID)
  2. chimw.RealIP                      -- rewrites r.RemoteAddr from X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP
  3. TracingMiddleware                 -- otelhttp span "http.request" wrapping the rest
  4. LoggerMiddleware                  -- wraps ResponseWriter (chimw.NewWrapResponseWriter), defers
                                          status/bytes/duration/request_id zerolog emission
  5. RecoveryMiddleware                -- defer recover(); on panic logs stack and writes
                                          http.StatusInternalServerError via writeError(w,500,...)
  6. ErrorTrackingMiddleware           -- centralized error aggregation (separate concern)
  7. PrometheusMiddleware              -- HTTP request metrics (duration, count, size)
  8. chimw.Compress(5)                 -- gzip/deflate response body
  9. cors.Handler(...)                 -- CORS, AllowCredentials only when explicit origin
 10. SecurityHeadersMiddleware
 11. http.MaxBytesReader(1<<20)        -- 1MB request body cap (a "DoS guard" already in place)

key_observations:
  - LoggerMiddleware ALREADY uses chimw.NewWrapResponseWriter and a deferred function
    that reads ww.Status() and ww.BytesWritten() AFTER the handler runs. The new
    APICallLogMiddleware MUST follow the same pattern (defer + WrapResponseWriter)
    so it observes the final status code and byte count, including the 500 emitted
    by RecoveryMiddleware on panic.
  - RecoveryMiddleware writes the 500 response itself (via writeError) and does not
    re-panic. So a middleware mounted ABOVE RecoveryMiddleware (closer to the entry)
    will see status=500 in its deferred read, and its own defer will also run.
  - chimw.GetReqID(r.Context()) yields the per-request UUID; that value is the right
    source for any "request_id" column we want to populate later.
  - LoggerMiddleware sets the X-Response-Time response header inside its defer; this
    proves writes to ww.Header() after next.ServeHTTP are still flushed before the
    final write of the response trailers when chunked, but for our purposes we only
    enqueue a record (no ResponseWriter mutation) -- safer.

